# Code standards

## General

- Prefer clear names and short functions; add a one-line docstring for any public function or class that is part of the library or benchmark API.
- Use type hints for function arguments and returns in new or modified code (`def f(x: pd.DataFrame) -> Dict[str, Any]:`).
- Handle edge cases (empty data, single batch) without silent wrong results; log or raise as appropriate.
- Do not introduce new dependencies without updating `pyproject.toml` and noting in progress/README if it affects reproducibility.

## When adding features (e.g. baselines)

- Before implementing, check `docs/BASELINE_COMPARISON_PROGRESS.md`: implement only what is in the plan; keep "same base model" for all comparison methods.
- New policy or runner code should be testable: add a small test or benchmark smoke test that runs the new path (e.g. one batch, TTA vs always-retrain) and asserts no crash and same model type.
- Benchmark scripts: same random seed and data splits for all methods so comparison is fair.

## Tests and benchmarks

- Do not break existing tests (`pytest tests/`). If a change requires updating a test, update the test and keep the intended behavior.
- New baseline code should reuse the existing base model (e.g. `TimeSeriesTransformer`) and data pipeline; only the update/adaptation logic should differ.
